\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 2.4 Operating Environment\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The client shall support current desktop and mobile versions of major browsers. The\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 server shall run in a hardened cloud or institutional environment using HTTPS.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Processing services may use containerized Python/ML workloads and a relational\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 database; object storage may retain approved screenshot artifacts.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  All data in transit shall use TLS; insecure HTTP callbacks are prohibited.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  The UI shall never render a submitted URL as an automatically clickable link before\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 warning the user.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Analysis models must expose a version identifier with each result.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Image size, file type, retention, and throughput shall be constrained to reduce abuse.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 2.6 Assumptions and Dependencies\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 CyberShield assumes that users submit content they are authorized to inspect and that\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 external reputation sources can be unavailable, stale, or incomplete. A network\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 connection is required for intelligence lookups. OCR accuracy depends on screenshot\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 resolution, language, compression, and visual complexity.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Dependencies include an authentication provider, URL/domain reputation feeds, an\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 OCR engine, an NLP classifier or ruleset, secure object storage, database backups, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 operational monitoring. A failed dependency shall degrade a result with a visible\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 limitation instead of silently producing certainty.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 2.7 Out of Scope\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Automatic clicking, visiting, or credential submission to suspicious websites.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Forensic compromise confirmation on the user device.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Blocking messages at a telecom carrier or enterprise email gateway.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Legal adjudication or irreversible account actions based solely on a model score.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 2.8 Business Rules\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The system shall classify results as Low, Medium, High, or Critical. High and Critical\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 results must display a prominent safety warning. A result can be \u8220?Inconclusive\u8221? when\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 evidence is insufficient or a dependency is unavailable. Users may delete their history\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 subject to legal and institutional retention requirements.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3. Specific Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.1 Requirement Conventions\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Each mandatory requirement uses the form FR (functional requirement) or NFR\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 (non-functional requirement). \u8220?Shall\u8221? indicates a required behavior. Priority labels are\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Must, Should, or Could. Acceptance statements are intentionally testable.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.2 URL Analysis Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-01 (Must): The system shall accept a single URL as text and reject\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 malformed, empty, or oversize input with a clear message.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-02 (Must): The system shall normalize the URL, parse hostname and path\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 components, and display the normalized value used for analysis.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-03 (Must): The system shall evaluate lexical indicators including misleading\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 subdomains, IP-address hosts, excessive length, encoded characters, suspicious\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keywords, and homoglyph risk.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-04 (Must): The system shall query configured reputation and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 threat-intelligence sources without opening the target in the user\u8217?s browser.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-05 (Should): The system shall assess redirect-chain evidence through a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 sandboxed service where approved by policy.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-06 (Must): The result shall show risk level, confidence band, indicators,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 limitations, and safe next actions.\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Figure 2: URL Processing Pipeline\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Input URL\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Normalize\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and Parse\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Threat\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Signals\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Score and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Explain\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.3 SMS and Email Text Analysis Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-01 (Must): The system shall accept pasted SMS text, email subject/body text,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 or structured email fields when available.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-02 (Must): The system shall remove or mask configured personally sensitive\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 fields before persistent storage while preserving sufficient evidence for scoring.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-03 (Must): The NLP pipeline shall identify urgency, threats, reward promises,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 impersonation cues, credential/payment requests, suspicious links, and grammar or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 formatting anomalies.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-04 (Must): The system shall extract embedded URLs, phone numbers, email\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 addresses, currency/payment references, and named brands when present.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-05 (Must): The system shall compare claimed sender identity with content\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 claims and flag apparent brand impersonation when confidence exceeds the configured\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 threshold.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-06 (Should): The system shall support English initially and return a limitation\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 notice for unsupported or mixed-language input.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-07 (Must): The result shall quote only minimal, safely masked evidence\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 snippets and shall not expose secrets detected in the submission.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: Text analysis must preserve the difference between a suspicious\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 expression and a confirmed malicious event. The NLP service identifies cues such as\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 urgency, impersonation, credential requests, payment pressure, and unusual sender\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 claims, then links those cues to the exact masked evidence used in the explanation. It\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 also records language and OCR confidence where applicable, allowing future\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 evaluation to identify cases in which wording, dialect, or short message format affects\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 reliability.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.4 Screenshot Phishing Detection Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-01 (Must): The system shall accept PNG, JPEG, and WEBP screenshots\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 within configured size and resolution limits.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-02 (Must): The system shall validate file signature and decode image pixels\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 before analysis; files that fail validation shall be rejected.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-03 (Must): The image pipeline shall run OCR to obtain visible text and route\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 that text through the same text-analysis service where applicable.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-04 (Must): The pipeline shall detect visual phishing signals including login\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 forms, password/OTP prompts, payment prompts, brand-like logos, shortened/obscured\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 links, QR codes, and urgency banners.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-05 (Should): The system shall compare extracted domain/brand clues with\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 known legitimate domain patterns and identify likely lookalikes.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-06 (Must): The result shall identify which evidence originated from OCR,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 visual analysis, or linked text analysis, and shall state OCR confidence.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-07 (Must): The system shall strip executable metadata and never execute\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 embedded content.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: Image analysis is designed as a layered process. File validation\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 occurs before OCR or visual classification; decoded pixels, not browser-rendered\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 content, are used for analysis. OCR text, form-like layout, QR-code presence, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 logo-like visual patterns are separate evidence sources. The final explanation names\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 their origin and confidence so a user understands when a conclusion relies on clearly\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 readable text versus a weaker visual resemblance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.5 Cross-Modal Scoring and Results\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-01 (Must): The scoring engine shall combine applicable indicators using\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 configurable, versioned weights or an approved model.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-02 (Must): The engine shall avoid double-counting the same extracted URL or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 claim across modalities.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-03 (Must): Each result shall include a score from 0 to 100, risk label,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 confidence band, reasons, model/rule version, and analysis timestamp.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-04 (Must): A Critical result shall recommend that the user avoid interacting\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 with the content, verify through an independently known channel, and report according\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 to local policy.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-05 (Must): An Inconclusive result shall identify unavailable sources or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 low-quality evidence and recommend a safer verification path.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-06 (Should): An authorized analyst may override a classification with an\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 explanation; overrides shall be immutable audit events.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Risk Interpretation\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 0-24 Low: no strong known indicators. 25-49 Medium: caution warranted. 50-74 High:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 likely suspicious. 75-100 Critical: strong indicators or known malicious evidence.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Scores are decision support, not proof of fraud.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.6 Account, History, and Feedback Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ACC-01 (Must): Registered users shall be able to create an account, sign in, sign\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 out, reset credentials through the approved identity mechanism, and manage consent\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 preferences.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ACC-02 (Must): The system shall display analysis history only to its owner or an\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 authorized role.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ACC-03 (Must): A user shall be able to delete an individual history item or request\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 deletion of eligible history records.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-FDB-01 (Must): Users shall be able to mark a result as helpful, unhelpful, suspected\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 false positive, or suspected false negative.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-FDB-02 (Should): Feedback intended for learning shall be reviewed or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 de-identified before it is used to tune a model or rule.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-RPT-01 (Must): The system shall generate a shareable incident summary containing\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 safe, masked indicators and no raw credentials.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-RPT-02 (Should): The system shall export authorized summaries in PDF or CSV\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 format with an export audit event.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.7 Administration and Governance Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ADM-01 (Must): Administrators shall manage user roles, retention periods,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 permitted file types, maximum file sizes, and enabled intelligence sources.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ADM-02 (Must): Administrators shall manage scoring thresholds and model/rule\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 releases through a change-controlled configuration process.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ADM-03 (Must): The system shall preserve a versioned audit event for\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 administrative changes, analyst overrides, exports, deletion actions, and sign-in failures.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ADM-04 (Should): Administrators shall view aggregated metrics for volume, risk\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 distribution, processing failures, feedback trend, and service latency.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ADM-05 (Must): Only authorized administrators shall access raw retained\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 screenshots, and all such access shall be logged.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-ADM-06 (Must): The administrative interface shall provide a health view that\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 distinguishes service failure, rate limiting, model unavailability, and intelligence-feed\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 degradation.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.8 External Interface Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 User Interface\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The interface shall offer clearly separated Analyze URL, Analyze Text, and Analyze\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Screenshot actions. Upload and submit buttons shall state what data will be processed.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The result shall show a risk badge, an evidence list, limitations, and safe actions. Color\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 shall not be the only way a risk level is conveyed.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Software Interfaces\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The API shall expose authenticated endpoints for submission, result retrieval, history,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 feedback, and administration. It shall use JSON over HTTPS. External feeds shall be\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 accessed through server-side adapters so credentials and provider-specific behavior are\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 not exposed to clients.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Hardware Interfaces\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 No special client hardware is required. Camera capture is outside the baseline; users\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 upload existing images. Servers may use CPU or approved GPU workers for OCR and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 image models.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Communications Interfaces\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The service shall use HTTPS with modern TLS. SMTP is not required in the baseline.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Notifications, if added, shall use a vetted provider and never include raw suspicious\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 content in a notification preview.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.9 Performance Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PERF-01 (Must): For a typical URL or text submission, the system shall return an\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 initial result within 5 seconds at the 95th percentile under the defined baseline load.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PERF-02 (Must): For a screenshot up to the configured maximum, the system\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 shall return a result within 15 seconds at the 95th percentile, excluding a displayed\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 external-feed timeout.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PERF-03 (Must): The system shall support at least 50 concurrent interactive\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 analyses in the academic deployment baseline without data cross-contamination.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PERF-04 (Should): Long-running tasks shall be queued and expose a request\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 status rather than holding a browser connection indefinitely.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PERF-05 (Must): A result page shall render with accessible core content before\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 optional charts or enrichment complete.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Capacity and Limits\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Initial limits are: URL 4,096 characters; text 25,000 characters; screenshot 10 MB;\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 history retention configurable from 0 to 180 days. Limits are configurable\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 administrative controls and must be visible in the UI.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.10 Security and Privacy Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-01 (Must): The system shall enforce role-based access control using least\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 privilege.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-02 (Must): The system shall encrypt data in transit and encrypt stored\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 sensitive data using approved platform controls.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-03 (Must): The system shall protect against common web threats including\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 injection, cross-site scripting, request forgery, insecure file upload, and broken\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 authorization.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-04 (Must): Credentials, API keys, session tokens, and detected secrets shall\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 not appear in application logs or result pages.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-05 (Must): Screenshot uploads shall be malware-scanned where\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 infrastructure permits, stored outside the web root, and accessed through time-limited\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 authorization.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PRV-01 (Must): The system shall minimize collection, explain retention at\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 submission, and honor approved deletion requests.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PRV-02 (Must): Analytics shall use aggregated or de-identified data where\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 practical.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-06 (Should): High-risk administrator actions shall require re-authentication\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 or multi-factor authentication.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: Security controls are evaluated as part of normal product\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 behavior rather than treated as a final deployment task. Each access decision, sensitive\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 export, analyst override, and configuration change must be attributable to an actor and a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 correlation identifier. Retention settings apply to raw input, derived indicators, assets,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and backups separately. This design supports review without retaining more personal\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 content than is necessary for the platform purpose.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.11 Reliability, Usability, and Accessibility\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-REL-01 (Must): The service shall fail safely. When a dependency fails, it shall\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 identify the limitation and not label content safe solely because a lookup failed.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-REL-02 (Must): Daily backups of required structured data shall be verifiable, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 restoration shall be tested at least once per release cycle.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-USE-01 (Must): The result explanation shall use concise, non-technical wording\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 with expandable technical detail.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-USE-02 (Must): The system shall not use fear-inducing or absolute language such\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 as \u8220?guaranteed safe.\u8221?\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-ACC-01 (Must): Keyboard navigation, visible focus, text alternatives, sufficient\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 contrast, and readable risk labels shall be supported.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-MNT-01 (Must): Rules, model artifacts, and integrations shall be versioned and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 traceable to a release record.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-LOC-01 (Could): Future releases may support additional languages, locale-aware\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 phishing examples, and right-to-left layouts.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 3.12 Data Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Table 5: Core Data Entities\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Entity\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Key fields\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Purpose\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 User\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 user_id, role, consent, created_at\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Identity and authorization.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 AnalysisRequest\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 request_id, modality, submitted_at, status\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Tracks an analysis job.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 AnalysisResult\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 result_id, score, label, version, created_at\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Stores outcome and evidence summary.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Indicator\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 indicator_id, type, value_hash, severity\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Stores a normalized signal safely.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 ScreenshotAsset\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 asset_id, object_ref, hash, OCR_confidence\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Controlled image metadata.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Feedback\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 feedback_id, result_id, category, note\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Captures user feedback.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 AuditEvent\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 event_id, actor, action, timestamp\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Immutable governance evidence.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Data Lifecycle\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Raw input is accepted, validated, analyzed, and either discarded or retained according\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 to consent and policy. Derived indicators are preferred over raw content for analytical\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 history. Screenshot storage uses a separate protected object reference; the database\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 stores only the minimum link and integrity hash.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4. System Models\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.1 Use Case Model\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Primary actor: User. Supporting actors: Analyst, Administrator, External Intelligence\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Provider, OCR Service. The user submits one of the three supported modalities and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 receives a result; the analyst reviews flagged cases; the administrator configures policy\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and inspects operational health.\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Figure 3: High-Level Use Case Flow\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Submit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Input\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Validate and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Analyze\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 View Risk\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Explanation\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Feedback or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Report\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.2 Model Assumptions\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Models identify information flow and responsibility; they do not prescribe a specific\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 framework or database vendor. All inter-service calls are authenticated and observable.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.3 Data Flow Model\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The client sends input to the API gateway. The gateway validates identity, rate limits,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and input type. It routes URL, text, and image requests to the relevant processor. The\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 score engine aggregates normalized indicators; the result service stores an approved\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 record and returns an explanation. External feeds provide enrichments but never receive\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 unnecessary user identity data.\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Figure 4: Data Flow\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Web Client\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 API Gateway\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 URL / NLP /\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Image Services\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Risk Engine\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Results and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Audit Store\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.4 Trust Boundaries\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The browser, application tier, worker tier, external feeds, and storage tier are distinct\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 trust zones. Input validation occurs at the boundary. Secrets remain server-side. Raw\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 assets never cross into public static hosting.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.5 Entity Relationship View\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A User owns zero or more AnalysisRequests. Each request produces zero or one\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 AnalysisResult during processing; a result contains one or more Indicators and can\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 receive zero or more Feedback records. ScreenshotAsset is optional and linked only for\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 image modality. AuditEvent references an actor and a protected action target.\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Figure 5: Entity Relationship View\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 User\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Request\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Result\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Indicator\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Event\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.6 Sequence View\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The submit sequence is: user submits input; client receives a request identifier; gateway\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 validates and dispatches; processors produce evidence; scoring creates the decision; the\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 result is persisted; the client displays the explanation. If a source times out, the\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 processor returns an explicit unavailable signal and scoring applies the uncertainty\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 policy.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.7 State Model\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 AnalysisRequest states are Created, Validating, Queued, Processing, Completed,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Inconclusive, Failed, and Deleted. A request may transition to Inconclusive after\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 permitted enrichment timeouts; it may transition to Failed only for technical or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 invalid-input reasons. Completed results remain immutable except for an authorized\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 analyst disposition or user feedback, each recorded separately.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.8 Error Handling Model\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Invalid input yields a client-safe validation error. A temporary service failure yields a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 retryable response with a correlation identifier. A policy violation yields a clear\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 explanation without disclosing security-control internals. Security events are logged in\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 a protected audit channel.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 4.9 Risk Decision Model\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The decision model uses evidence quality, severity, corroboration, and source\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 reliability. It must distinguish absent evidence from unavailable evidence. A high score\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 from one reliable malicious-domain source may be sufficient, whereas weak linguistic\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 signals alone should yield cautious wording and appropriate confidence.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5. Verification and Acceptance\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5.1 Requirements Validation\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Validation shall combine review, automated tests, integration tests, security tests,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 usability checks, and controlled test datasets. Test data shall not include live malicious\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 payloads, active credentials, or unauthorized personal information.\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Table 6: Sample Traceability Matrix\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Requirement\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Verification method\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Acceptance evidence\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-URL-03\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Unit and integration tests\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Indicators returned for curated URLs.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-TXT-03\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NLP test corpus and review\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Urgency and credential-request cues identified.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-IMG-03\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 OCR fixture tests\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Expected text and confidence recorded.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 FR-SCR-03\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 API contract test\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Score, label, reasons, version returned.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-SEC-05\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Security review and test\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Rejected unsafe upload; protected asset path.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NFR-PERF-02\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Load test\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 95th percentile screenshot latency report.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5.2 Acceptance Criteria\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  All Must requirements have passing verification evidence or an approved,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 documented exception.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  No Critical or High result displays a clickable suspicious link by default.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  No test reveals raw credentials or secrets in UI, logs, or export.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  The final demo can analyze representative URL, text, and screenshot cases end to end.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: Verification evidence should demonstrate both successful and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 degraded behavior. Test cases therefore include valid samples, malformed inputs,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 unavailable feeds, low-quality screenshots, denied authorization requests, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 scoring-policy changes. The team records expected result labels as well as expected\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 limitations, because a system that explains an unavailable dependency correctly is safer\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 than one that silently produces an apparently certain assessment.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5.3 Test Strategy\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Unit tests cover parsers, normalizers, score calculations, masking, authorization rules,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and storage adapters. Integration tests cover API-to-worker flow, feed timeouts, OCR\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 handoff, and audit events. End-to-end tests use approved benign and synthetic phishing\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 examples. Performance tests measure concurrent submissions and queue behavior.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5.4 Security Testing\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Security testing includes dependency review, authentication and authorization tests,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 upload validation, API schema validation, rate-limit tests, logging inspection, and a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 review against relevant OWASP risks. Testing must be authorized and confined to\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 owned systems.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5.5 Model Evaluation\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The project shall record precision, recall, false-positive rate, false-negative rate, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 latency for representative labeled datasets. Scores must be interpreted with dataset\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 limitations. Model updates require regression testing against a held-out approved set\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and review of changed error patterns.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 5.6 Usability Evaluation\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 At least a small representative user review shall verify that users can submit each\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 modality, understand the risk label, find the evidence, and identify a safe next step.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Feedback must not be used to claim statistical validity unless the study design supports\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 it.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: Verification evidence should demonstrate both successful and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 degraded behavior. Test cases therefore include valid samples, malformed inputs,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 unavailable feeds, low-quality screenshots, denied authorization requests, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 scoring-policy changes. The team records expected result labels as well as expected\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 limitations, because a system that explains an unavailable dependency correctly is safer\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 than one that silently produces an apparently certain assessment.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 6. Deployment and Operations\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 6.1 Deployment Architecture\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A recommended deployment separates the browser client, application API,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 asynchronous worker queue, URL/NLP/image processors, database, protected object\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 storage, and monitoring stack. Environment-specific configuration and secrets are\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 injected securely; they are never committed to source control.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 6.2 Monitoring and Logging\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Operational metrics include request count by modality, success/error rate, latency\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 percentiles, queue depth, dependency status, model version distribution, and storage\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 growth. Security logs include authentication failures, authorization denials,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 administrative changes, sensitive exports, and protected asset access. Logs must be\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 integrity protected and retention limited.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 6.3 Incident Handling\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 When CyberShield itself is suspected of compromise, administrators shall contain\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 access, preserve authorized evidence, rotate relevant credentials, assess affected data,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 restore service safely, and document the event. Users should be informed through\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 approved channels when disclosure is required.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 6.4 Maintenance\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Routine maintenance covers dependency patches, threat-feed health, model drift\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 checks, backup verification, capacity review, and periodic access review. A rollback\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 path is required for scoring rules and model releases.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 7. Appendices\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 Appendix A: Sample Result Structure\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A result contains: request identifier; modality; normalized input summary; risk score\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and label; confidence; detected indicators with severity and evidence source; safe\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recommendations; limitations; created time; rule/model version; and retention/deletion\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 controls.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 Appendix B: Safe User Guidance\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Do not click or reply when a message demands urgent credential, OTP, or payment\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 action.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Verify using a trusted official app, bookmarked site, or independently known phone\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 number.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Do not share passwords, one-time codes, recovery codes, or card details.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Report suspicious content through the appropriate organization or service channel.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 Appendix C: Future Enhancements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Potential future work includes multilingual NLP, QR-code destination analysis,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 email-header parsing, explainability improvements, organization-specific brand\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 profiles, browser extension integration, and SOC/ticketing integration. Each\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 enhancement requires a separate privacy and security assessment.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 Appendix D: Functional Requirement Summary\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Table 7: Requirement Groups\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Group\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Purpose\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Priority\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 URL analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Detect technical and reputation-based URL risk indicators.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Must\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Text analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Identify phishing intent and entities in SMS/email text.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Must\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Screenshot analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Combine OCR and visual cues to assess phishing\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 screenshots.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Must\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Scoring\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Produce an explainable, calibrated risk decision.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Must\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Governance\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Control roles, configuration, audit, and retention.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Must\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Reporting\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Provide safe summaries, exports, and user feedback.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Should\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Interpretation Note\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The requirements are deliberately layered: validation and safe storage are prerequisites\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 for analysis; modality-specific evidence is normalized before it reaches scoring; the\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 result experience then explains uncertainty and recommends a safe action. This\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 approach makes each component testable without treating any one signal as definitive.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Configuration Baseline\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Thresholds, enabled feeds, retention duration, supported languages, maximum inputs,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and model versions are configuration values. They must be change controlled and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recorded with each analysis result so the behavior can be reproduced during review.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 Appendix E: Example Indicators\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 URL Indicators\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Domain contains a suspicious lookalike of a known brand.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Hostname is a raw IP address or uses misleading nested subdomains.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  URL includes excessive encoding, unusual path structure, or credential/payment\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 terms.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Configured intelligence source reports a malicious or suspicious reputation.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Text Indicators\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Artificial urgency, account threat, prize, refund, or unexpected payment language.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Request for password, OTP, recovery code, card detail, or remote-access action.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Impersonation claim that conflicts with sender clues or embedded domain.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Obscured link, unusual contact channel, or abrupt instruction to move conversation.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Image Indicators\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  OCR extracts a login form, password/OTP request, or payment prompt.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Visual structure imitates a brand while domains or text show mismatches.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  QR code or shortened link attempts to conceal destination information.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1  Low image quality reduces OCR confidence and must reduce certainty.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 Appendix F: Risk Communication Examples\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Low Risk\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 \u8220?No strong phishing indicators were found in this analysis. This does not confirm that\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 the content is safe. Continue only if you expected it and can verify the sender or website\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 independently.\u8221?\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Medium Risk\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 \u8220?This content includes signals often used in scams, such as urgency or an unfamiliar\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 link. Do not provide credentials or payment information. Verify through an official\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 channel before taking action.\u8221?\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 High / Critical Risk\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 \u8220?Multiple strong phishing indicators were detected. Avoid clicking, replying, scanning\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 a QR code, or sharing information. Use a known official website or contact method to\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 verify the request and report it if appropriate.\u8221?\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Inconclusive\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 \u8220?The analysis could not use one or more evidence sources, or the image/text quality\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 was limited. Treat the content cautiously and verify through an independently trusted\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 channel.\u8221?\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 These examples are guidance templates, not legal or incident-response instructions. The\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 final user interface shall show the specific reasons behind each classification.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8. Detailed Architecture Requirements\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.1 Client and API Layer\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The browser client is responsible for collecting input, presenting consent language,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 showing progress, and rendering the completed assessment. It must not execute URLs,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 decode unknown files through browser plug-ins, or embed untrusted submitted content\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 as active HTML. The API layer is responsible for authentication, validation, rate\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 limiting, request identifiers, routing, response shaping, and audit correlation. It is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 deliberately kept separate from resource-intensive analysis workers so a high-cost\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 screenshot cannot prevent normal account or result operations.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.2 Asynchronous Worker Layer\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Analysis workers consume validated jobs from a queue. A job contains a request\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 identifier, an input reference, a modality, a privacy policy identifier, and a trace\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 context; it does not require the worker to know the user password or browser session.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Workers return normalized indicators, evidence confidence, processing status, and an\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 analyzer version. The score engine receives these normalized outputs and applies a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 consistent decision policy. This separation permits independent scaling of URL, NLP,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and image workloads.\par
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.3 Storage Layer\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Structured records are kept in the database with foreign-key and authorization controls.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Large image assets are stored in protected object storage under an opaque object\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 reference. Access to an object must be mediated by an authorization check and a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 short-lived retrieval mechanism. A content hash supports integrity checks and duplicate\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 detection without retaining unnecessary copies. Backups must be encrypted, tested, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 governed by the same retention schedule as the primary store.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation Notes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Implementation note: This requirement group is implemented through explicit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 interfaces, versioned configuration, and measurable acceptance criteria. The design\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 keeps input validation, evidence collection, risk scoring, explanation, and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 recording distinct so that a change in one component can be reviewed and tested\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 without unintentionally changing every result. Where uncertainty exists, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 states the limitation and provides a safe verification action instead of presenting a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 binary assurance.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.4 URL Analysis Design Detail\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 URL analysis begins with deterministic parsing. The system records scheme, hostname,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 effective top-level domain, path, query, port, and selected normalized forms. It\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 identifies transformations that conceal intent, such as percent-encoding, repeated\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 delimiters, embedded user-info fields, punycode, or a long path crafted to hide the real\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 host. The parser shall not treat a visual resemblance as an established identity; it reports\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 it as a lookalike indicator with an explanation.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The reputation adapter queries approved sources by domain, URL, hash, or IP\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 according to the source contract. Timeouts and provider errors are represented as\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 source-unavailable indicators. A provider response has a source name, fetch time,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 policy-approved confidence, and response classification. Cached reputation data is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 marked with age so a user and analyst can understand whether evidence is fresh. The\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 platform must never transmit a user identifier, personal note, or full unrelated message\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 body to a URL intelligence provider.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 When sandboxed redirect evaluation is enabled, the worker uses isolated networking\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and strict time, size, and hop limits. The returned output contains only the final\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 normalized domains, redirect count, and selected safety indicators. It must not\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 download unknown executable content, interact with forms, or attempt credential\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 submission. These controls make URL enrichment an observation process rather than\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 an active browsing action.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.5 NLP Analysis Design Detail\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The text service first separates message parts into subject, sender label, body, quoted\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 reply, and attachment description when those fields are present. It normalizes\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 whitespace, common obfuscation, Unicode variants, and repeated punctuation while\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 retaining an original protected copy only when policy permits. Tokenization and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 sentence segmentation provide input to rules and classifier features. The service detects\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 requests for secrets, money, account verification, delivery confirmation, remote access,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 or rapid action; it also detects coercive language such as threats of account closure.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Entity extraction identifies URLs, email addresses, telephone numbers, payment\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 handles, organization names, dates, and amounts. A claimed organization is compared\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 with text surrounding the request and any embedded domains. The comparison\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 produces an explanation such as \u8220?message claims to be from a bank but directs you to\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 an unrelated domain,\u8221? rather than an unsupported accusation. Where confidence is low,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 the result says that the wording is unusual or cannot be verified.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 NLP output is not a substitute for human language understanding in every context.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Legitimate operational messages may use urgent wording, and phishing messages may\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 be grammatically polished. Therefore, the engine combines linguistic evidence with\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 link, sender, or image evidence, applies conservative labels to isolated weak cues, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 captures evaluation metrics by language and content type.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.6 Image and OCR Analysis Design Detail\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Screenshot analysis starts with file validation and pixel decoding. The service checks\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 the declared type against the file signature, measures dimensions, and rejects image\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 bombs or unsupported color formats. It removes nonessential metadata from the\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 working derivative. The protected original is retained only if consent and retention\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 policy allow it. A thumbnail used by analysts must be generated from the safe decoded\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 pixels rather than served directly from the original upload.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 OCR produces text blocks, confidence values, and approximate coordinates. The text\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 blocks feed the NLP service, while layout logic uses coordinates to recognize forms,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 input labels, submit buttons, QR codes, browser-like address bars, and warning\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 banners. Visual detection may also estimate whether a logo or color pattern appears\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 brand-like; it cannot establish ownership or authenticity by itself. Such cues are scored\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 as corroborative observations.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The user result shall make the provenance of each signal clear. For example, \u8220?OCR\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 detected the phrase \u8216?enter your one-time code\u8217? with high text confidence\u8221? is distinct\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 from \u8220?the image contains a logo-like graphic.\u8221? If OCR confidence is poor, the platform\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 lowers the certainty of text-derived conclusions and prompts the user to verify\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 manually rather than creating a falsely precise score.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.7 Risk-Scoring Methodology\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The scoring service is an explicit policy component. It receives normalized indicators\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 with source reliability, severity, freshness, modality, and confidence. Before calculation\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 it merges duplicate evidence: a URL extracted from a screenshot and the same URL\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 pasted in the text is one indicator with multi-modal corroboration, not two unrelated\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 threats. Severity weights represent potential harm and evidence reliability; confidence\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 reflects how well a signal was observed.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A rules-first baseline is suitable for the academic release because it can be reviewed and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 tested deterministically. A future trained model may provide a bounded score\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 contribution when it has documented training data, evaluation results, drift monitoring,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and a rollback path. In either approach, thresholds are versioned configuration. A\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 decision record must preserve the score inputs, the applied policy version, and the final\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 label so retrospective review is possible.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Calibration checks are required. A score of 70 must have a stable meaning across\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 modalities and must not result merely from a long message containing many weak\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 features. The team shall review false positives and false negatives by modality, adjust\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 thresholds under change control, and test representative clean, ambiguous, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 malicious examples before release.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.8 User Experience and Safety Content\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The first screen should explain that CyberShield analyzes submitted material for signs\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 of phishing and that no automated tool can prove complete safety. The three analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 methods are presented as equal choices so users are not led to assume phishing exists\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 only in URLs. Submission controls include clear examples, input limits, privacy\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 information, and a visible option to remove an uploaded asset after the current analysis\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 when policy allows.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A result page has four reading levels: the risk label and immediate safe action; a short\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 explanation; a detailed list of indicators; and technical metadata for authorized users.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 High-risk pages keep the safe action near the top: avoid clicking, do not enter\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 credentials or codes, verify using an independently known channel, and report through\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 a suitable process. The visual design uses text labels and icons as well as color. It does\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 not use panic language, flashing warnings, or a false \u8220?safe\u8221? badge.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 When a user supplies feedback, the form distinguishes \u8220?the explanation was unclear\u8221?\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 from \u8220?the classification seems wrong.\u8221? It warns users not to add passwords, OTPs,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 banking information, or personal identity documents in the feedback note. This avoids\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 turning an improvement pathway into a secondary data-collection risk.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.9 Authorization and Audit Detail\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Authorization is evaluated on every server-side action. A registered user can see only\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 their own current and historical analyses. An analyst can access assigned or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 policy-authorized flagged cases but does not automatically receive administration\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 rights. Administrators configure the system but are still subject to audit when viewing\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 sensitive artifacts. Auditors receive a read-only view of permitted logs and reports. The\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 role model must deny access by default and use server-side checks, not only hidden\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 user-interface controls.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Audit events record the actor identifier or service identity, event type, target type, target\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 identifier, time, correlation identifier, result, and limited contextual metadata. They do\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 not record passwords, raw session tokens, or unrestricted original message content.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Events are append-only from the application point of view; changes to audit storage are\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 restricted and monitored. The audit trail supports investigation of configuration\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 changes, access to raw screenshots, exports, deletions, analyst overrides, and repeated\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 sign-in failures.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The service accounts used by OCR, reputation, and storage adapters have minimum\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 permissions. Secrets are stored in an approved secret manager or protected deployment\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 configuration and are rotated on a planned schedule or immediately after suspected\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 exposure.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.10 Privacy, Retention, and Ethical Use\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 CyberShield processes content that can contain private conversations, contact details,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 financial references, and screenshots of accounts. The product therefore uses data\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 minimization: only input required for the selected analysis is collected, raw data is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 retained only when permitted, and derived evidence is preferred for history and metrics.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A clear notice explains that submitted content may be inspected by authorized\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 personnel if the user consents or policy requires it.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Retention is a configurable policy rather than a hidden implementation detail. The\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 baseline distinguishes transient processing artifacts, user-visible history, aggregated\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 metrics, and protected security audit records. Each class has a purpose, owner, retention\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 duration, deletion method, and approved exceptions. On expiration, stored raw assets\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and content are deleted or irreversibly de-identified. Backup expiration is documented\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 so deletion requests are handled consistently.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The project must avoid using private user submissions to train models by default. If a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 future learning program is approved, it should use de-identified, consented, reviewed\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 data with access restrictions. Human reviewers should see the least information\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 required to evaluate a case and should receive guidance on handling sensitive or\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 distressing content.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.11 Detailed Test Cases\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\b\fs22 Table 8: Representative Acceptance Cases\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Case\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Input and action\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Expected outcome\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 TC-01\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Paste a malformed URL.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Validation message; no analysis job is created.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 TC-02\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Submit a known-safe synthetic domain.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Low or inconclusive result with stated limitations;\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 no automatic navigation.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 TC-03\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Submit synthetic SMS requesting an OTP via\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 a suspicious link.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Text cues, extracted link, High/Critical guidance,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 masked evidence.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 TC-04\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Upload a synthetic login screenshot with a QR\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 code.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 File validates; OCR and visual indicators reported\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 with confidence.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 TC-05\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Force a reputation adapter timeout.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Result is inconclusive or reduced-confidence;\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 timeout is explained.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 TC-06\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Attempt cross-user history access.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Server returns authorization denial and audit\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 event.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Test data will use fictional domains, synthetic messages, and authorized image fixtures.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 No test shall distribute active malicious links, credential harvesters, or private user data.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Regression suites should run for every scoring-policy, model, parser, or dependency\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 update.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.12 Deployment, Release, and Rollback\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The release process separates development, test, staging, and production-like\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 environments. Configuration values and external credentials differ by environment and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 must never be copied into source code. Before release, the team reviews unit and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 integration results, dependency vulnerabilities, schema migrations, model/rule version,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 performance evidence, backup status, and operational runbook updates.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 A release is reversible. Rule and model updates are labeled with a unique version,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 tested against an approved regression set, and deployed in a way that allows the prior\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 version to be restored. Database migrations include a backup and rollback decision.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Monitoring thresholds are checked immediately after deployment for request errors,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 latency, queue depth, unexpected score distribution, and external-feed failures.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Changes that affect personal-data retention, analyst access, or third-party sharing\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 require privacy and security review before deployment. Emergency changes follow a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 documented exception path, including a post-change review and complete audit record.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.13 Operational Runbook Summary\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Normal operations begin with health checks for the API, queue, processors, database,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 object storage, and intelligence adapters. A degraded external feed is not automatically\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 a platform outage; the system continues with a visible limitation if the remaining\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 evidence is sufficient. A database, authentication, or storage authorization failure is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 handled as a service-impacting event because it may affect safety or privacy.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 When a worker backlog grows, operators check input volume, processing latency, error\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 rate, worker availability, and unusually large image submissions. Capacity can be\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 increased by adding isolated worker instances subject to rate limits and cost controls.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Operators do not inspect raw user inputs unless that access is necessary, authorized, and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 logged.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 The runbook includes contacts, escalation conditions, backup restoration steps,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 model/rule rollback steps, security incident handling, and a user-facing status\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 communication template. Periodic drills validate that the team can restore a backup,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 disable a faulty intelligence adapter, and recover from a failed model release without\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 exposing data or returning misleading \u8220?safe\u8221? decisions.\par
\page
\pard\sb240\sa140\b\fs32 8.14 Expanded Glossary\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Brand impersonation: use of a name, image, or wording intended to appear associated\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 with an organization. Confidence: an estimate of evidence quality, not the probability\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 that a user will be harmed. Domain: the registered internet name used to identify a host.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Homoglyph: a character that visually resembles another character. Lookalike domain: a\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 domain crafted to resemble a legitimate one. OCR confidence: the engine\u8217?s estimate\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 that extracted image text is correct.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Protected asset: a stored uploaded image available only through authorized retrieval.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Reputation feed: an external source that provides reported risk information about an\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 indicator. Risk label: the user-facing category derived from score and policy.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Sandboxed evaluation: constrained inspection performed in an isolated environment.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Signal provenance: the source and method through which evidence was obtained.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 Threat intelligence: curated data about malicious infrastructure or behavior.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 User-visible explanation: short wording that states why the result was produced and\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 what to do next. Versioned policy: a named, immutable release of thresholds, weights,\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 and decision rules. This glossary is normative where a term is used in a requirement;\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 implementation documentation may add vendor-specific definitions without changing\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl360\slmult1 these meanings.\par
